@.str.48 = private unnamed_addr constant [36 x i8] c"verify the integrity of store paths\00", align 1
@.str.49 = private unnamed_addr constant [1192 x i8] c"\0A\0A# Examples\0A\0A* Verify the entire Nix store:\0A\0A  ```console\0A  # nix store verify --all\0A  ```\0A\0A* Check whether each path in the closure of Firefox has at least 2\0A  signatures:\0A\0A  ```console\0A  # nix store verify --recursive --sigs-needed 2 --no-contents $(type -p firefox)\0A  ```\0A\0A* Verify a store path in the binary cache `https://cache.nixos.org/`:\0A\0A  ```console\0A  # nix store verify --store https://cache.nixos.org/ \\\0A      /nix/store/v5sv61sszx301i0x6xysaqzla09nksnd-hello-2.10\0A  ```\0A\0A# Description\0A\0AThis command verifies the integrity of the store paths [*installables*](./nix.md#installables),\0Aor, if `--all` is given, the entire Nix store. For each path, it\0Achecks that\0A\0A* its contents match the NAR hash recorded in the Nix database; and\0A\0A* it is *trusted*, that is, it is signed by at least one trusted\0A  signing key, is content-addressed, or is built locally (\22ultimately\0A  trusted\22).\0A\0A# Exit status\0A\0AThe exit status of this command is the sum of the following values:\0A\0A* **1** if any path is corrupted (i.e. its contents don't match the\0A  recorded NAR hash).\0A\0A* **2** if any path is untrusted.\0A\0A* **4** if any path couldn't be verified for any other reason (such as\0A  an I/O error).\0A\0A\00", align 1
